Recent completed subject checkpoints (@ergon-history-3: 0, is #chapter) { r_000A7F91F776C8EC99F3D51372
Historical evidence recorded before the skeleton migration. Counts, pending statuses, temporary handles and next-step instructions describe that checkpoint; consult the current development Record before acting.

Water and sanitation is complete as authored educational material on 30 October 2026. Its 170 Records include forty sources, a source index and 129 learning Records; thirteen reused learning Records produce a 142-Record selected route. The final extension adds 32 learning Records and nine sources, developing industrial reuse, cooling/steam/condensate, high-purity service, controls, commissioning, maintenance, service chronology, restoration, continuing work, three advanced system studies and independent research. The selected graph branches into drinking-water, wastewater and industrial research, then converges at uncertainty and criticism. Books, articles, datasets and software remain external references. Educational completion demands actual sustained reader work; future laboratory studies and independent research are not claimed to have been performed by the author.

Independent final-extension checks passed for 324 reuse cases against exact rational balances and iterative mixing, 1,728 service histories against an independent discrete-volume simulation, 32 invalid-input cases, and cooling/steam/condensate/sample-line/reliability/cost examples. The tank-control discretization shows first-order convergence against the exact continuous solution. Earlier verified treatment, pressure/exposure, steady biological and batch-growth calculations remain unchanged. Six original programs are preserved by publication export. The final water publication test passed, as did the original electrical-power and thermal publication tests after adding the shared water continuations. Cargo check for the publication-enabled Ergon integration test and rustfmt checking passed without compiler warnings. Lingua checked all 1,750 Hephaestus files, comprising 1,788 Records, and the local-reference audit found no missing targets. The water planning leaf is removed; the developed UID is preserved. The remaining Hephaestus scope is 137 subject plans across sixteen field groups.

Final verification timings were 498.53 seconds for water, 144.60 seconds for electrical power and 195.36 seconds for thermodynamics. All three test processes completed successfully. After removing the water planning leaf, the independent register audit confirmed 264 planning/general Records, sixteen Hephaestus groups, 137 pending subject scopes, matching counts, 1,748 unique explicit developed Hephaestus identities and no identity collisions with the register. All 23,822 local references resolved. The historical cross-umbrella pending inventory has 185 entries. No source media or views directories were introduced.

The nine added primary sources are EPA/USAID's 2012 water-reuse guidance, CDC's cooling-tower module, WHO's 2021 pharmaceutical-water annex, NIST SP 800-82 revision 3, ANA's 2024 second-edition consumptive-use manual, EPA's 2022 asset handbook, EPA/USDA's 2016 utility guide, IWA's BSM2 technical report and Amusat and colleagues' 2024 scaling/optimization article. Their Records retain edition, rights and access distinctions. ANA's 83.6 MB book was retrieved outside the repo and its cover, contents and industrial chapter inspected. NIST's 2026 revision-4 initial draft is distinguished from the cited final revision 3. BSM2's public specification is distinguished from some requested implementation files. The scaling paper's OLI-generated chemistry data are a proprietary dependency; an open alternative is a changed-model comparison, not an exact reproduction claim.

Mechanical engineering is complete as authored educational material on 30 October 2026. Its 187 own Records contain 167 learning Records, nineteen sources and a source index. Eleven reused quantitative, metrology and metallurgy Records produce a 178-Record selected route. Six entries discover the 1,018-document composition. The original root UID `r_0017YZ63HZBZ4P4WD8DECVWE4R` is preserved; the separate ERG-120 planning leaf has been removed. Engineering has thirty pending subjects; the Hephaestus total is 136 across sixteen field groups.

The full route develops ordinary machine observations and bilingual vocabulary through statics, dynamics, analytical mechanics, vibration, material/structural behavior, machine elements, requirements, drawings, error budgets, manufacture, inspection, assembly, commissioning, operation, maintenance, failure investigation, repair and recovery. Four sets of three advanced studies converge through integration Records. The worked horizontal measuring-carriage account joins reflected inertia, compliance, a rejected accuracy claim, production/inspection planning, motion/energy calculation and reader construction/characterization. Scholarly criticism and independent research conclude the formation. These are substantial future reader tasks; the authored conceptual example does not claim a physically built prototype.

The final extension added 47 learning Records and seven sources: NPL Guide 79 (2005), Etchepare/Gravina's 17-page Portuguese drawing handout, MIT 2.008 Spring 2025, Hale's 493-page 1999 thesis, NASA's revision-2 systems handbook, Li and colleagues' 2024 thermal-error article and Vogl/Weiss/Helu's diagnostic/prognostic review. Actual selected passages, edition details and access/licence conditions were checked. The complete NPL, Portuguese and Hale PDFs were retrieved only outside the repository. NPL Crown copyright, publisher/author rights, OCW exceptions and CC BY-NC-ND restrictions remain distinguished from unrestricted reuse. Books, datasets, media and software remain external links.

The new shared `met-dimensional-case` and `metal-joining` Records preserve their original imports and route assertions, adding only mechanical continuations to `mech-inspection-decision` and `mech-final-material-state`. No reverse mechanical imports were added. Nine composed routes remain intact. Four inspectable original programs now cover beam reactions/sections, oscillator dynamics/energy, a thermoelastic axial bar and feed-axis motion/energy.

Independent final-extension verification extracted and ran the axis program from its Record in another working directory. It passed 432 high-precision analytical comparisons and reflected-drive scaling cases, phase quadrature and electrical energy balances, triangular/trapezoidal continuity, resistance-free returned energy and 34 invalid/unrepresentable input cases. The stated carriage example takes 5.2 s, reaches 240 rpm and requires 0.5 J net shaft work plus approximately 0.00831252 J copper loss under its declared idealizations. Dimensional stacks/correlation, thermal/Abbe errors, machining volume/speed/power, capability indices, production crossover, availability and stiffness examples were independently checked. Earlier verified mechanics/material/component examples are unchanged.

Cargo check for the publication-enabled Ergon test and rustfmt passed without compiler warnings. Lingua checked all 1,937 Hephaestus files, comprising 1,975 Records. The final mechanical publication test passed in 374.42 seconds, the original metallurgy test in 267.75 seconds and metrology in 11.67 seconds. Exact learning membership/reachability, six-entry discovery, nine route selections, URLs, internal learning locations and all four programs survived export, move and fresh reopening. After the completion edit, the register passed Lingua checking with 263 Records. The independent inventory confirmed sixteen Hephaestus groups, 136 pending subjects, 1,935 unique developed identities without planning collisions and 184 historical pending scopes across umbrellas. All 25,441 local Lingua references and 226 execution-ledger links resolved. No new Trail features, views or bundled source files were introduced.

Materials engineering, ERG-122, is complete as authored educational material at [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../../ergon/hephaestus/engineering/materials-engineering/Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9). Its 351 own Records comprise 301 learning Records, forty-nine sources and a source index. Seventy-two shared steps give 373 selected learning Records. Six-entry discovery composes 1,852 documents across thirteen subjects. Eight sets of three advanced studies lead into independent research, defense and revision. Final publication and relocation passed on 30 October 2026; the distinct pending planning leaf is retired, with the developed identity retained.

The structural opening develops everyday material questions, bilingual vocabulary, quantities, state/history, composition, properties, representative measurement, atomic interactions, electronic/chemical preparation, crystals, polymers/glasses, symmetry, anisotropy, defects, interfaces, microscopy, diffraction and source-model evidence. Equilibrium and kinetics develop potentials, chemical potentials, mixing, phase stability/diagrams, capillarity, nucleation, diffusion, moving interfaces, solidification, spinodal evolution, transformation histories, coarsening, sintering, recovery, thermal analysis and assessed calculations.

The family/mechanical section develops metals/alloy states, ceramics/refractories, glasses/glass ceramics, thermoplastics, networks/gels, elastomers, composites and natural/cellular materials. Shared mechanics and metallurgy lead into test interpretation, anisotropic elasticity, residual stress, hereditary/dynamic response, master curves, polymer nonlinearity, plasticity, strengthening, localization, fracture, toughening, brittle statistics, thermal shock, load transfer, laminates, damage, fatigue, creep and property inference. Three substantial studies require derivation, independent calculation, primary evidence, criticism and revision.

The new [From material structure to functional response](../../ergon/hephaestus/engineering/materials-engineering/functional/From-material-structure-to-functional-response.lingua#r_6JHQV78YK5QJAX1C718WZ9945P) adds 37 learning Records, eight source Records and ten shared continuations. Complex amplitudes, wavefunctions, confinement, oscillators, ensembles and occupation prepare the material's heat capacity, phonons, transport, bands and effective mass. Carrier neutrality, electronic/disordered/ionic transport and contacts lead into polarization, relaxation, loss, ferroelectricity, piezoelectricity, pyroelectric/thermoelectric coupling, optical constants/transitions/luminescence, magnetic moments/order/domains, soft/hard/spintronic applications and superconductivity. Measurement inference and three advanced studies join mechanisms with primary evidence and changed-condition predictions. Whole books, problem sequences and graduate work establish substantial depth; the introductory explanations do not substitute for those readings or for the separate full quantum/statistical/solid-state subjects.

The ten reused Records are `th-statistical`, `th-kinetic-transport`, `th-interfaces-nano`, `em-current-model`, `em-hall`, `em-polarization`, `em-matter-models`, `em-loss`, `em-magnetic-matter` and `th-coupled`. Each keeps its original imports and route assertions, adding only its materials continuation and ordinary explanatory link. No reverse discovery imports were added. All previous quantitative, chemistry, mineralogy, metallurgy and mechanical sharing remains intact. The MIT 2.57 transport and existing electromagnetic measurement sources retain their original identities.

Earlier sources include Zolin's 76-page Portuguese introduction; MIT 3.012/3.063; Cambridge structure, phase and mechanical packages; Cota's complete 488-page updated 2026 thermodynamics book; MIT 3.205/3.21; existing Bhadeshia/Yan, Bhadeshia teaching and pycalphad; the 2021 Wood Handbook; the publisher-hosted ceramic breviary; and existing Roylance, Bower and MIT 3.22. The handbook's institutional citation gives 543 pages. Official contents and chapter metadata were inspected, but its full PDF and tables were not inspected at that earlier checkpoint. Roylance's viscoelastic, laminate and fracture-statistics modules contain 38, 18 and 20 pages; selected derivations were inspected. The viscoelastic PDF is dated October 2001 despite the associated Fall 1999 course label.

The eight new sources are Tong's Quantum Mechanics (133 pages), Statistical Physics (191 pages) and Solid State Physics (131 pages); Simon's 269-page 2012 draft; MIT 3.23 Fall 2007; Ralph's 89-page Cornell graduate notes (2024); Cambridge's functional packages; and Lucy V. C. Assali's 2018 IFUSP Portuguese slides. Their official/author pages, contents and selected technical passages were inspected on 30 October 2026. Assali's name was checked visually because extraction garbles it; the first crystal file contains 33 pages and the semiconductor file 77. The slides complement the complete books. Simon's chapter 23 is explicitly unfinished and is not assigned as a complete magnetic-device treatment. MIT's magnetic lectures 19–23 are unavailable; Ralph supplies graduate magnetic/superconducting depth. MIT index topics and PDF titles can differ: inspected PDFs contain Bloch/reciprocal-lattice material (ten pages) and optical coefficients/oscillators (sixteen pages), with commercial-textbook dependencies and removed/separately credited images. Not every solution or interactive activity was exercised.

The functional section explicitly corrects the Cambridge semiconductor introduction's unqualified intrinsic mid-gap statement: in the nondegenerate parabolic-band model, μ_i=(Ec+Ev)/2+(kBT/2)ln(Nv/Nc). The exact midpoint requires equal effective densities of states. Earlier ceramic thermal-shock material also retains its independently derived, dimensionally consistent square-root coefficient rather than reproducing the source's faulty displayed relation. Source criticism is part of the reading work.

Access and adaptation rights remain distinct. Ralph and Bhadeshia/Yan explicitly provide CC BY 4.0 material; MIT/Cambridge use CC BY-NC-SA with exceptions. Free author/institutional reading of Tong, Simon, Assali, Cota, Zolin, Bower, the Wood Handbook and ceramic guide does not establish unrestricted rights to every component. All books, articles, software, media and datasets remain external references.

Five original standard-library Python examples are embedded in learning Records: cubic diffraction/basis interference, symmetric regular-solution separation, conservative sealed-slab diffusion, generalized-Maxwell loading history and [An inspectable band and occupation account](../../ergon/hephaestus/engineering/materials-engineering/computation/An-inspectable-band-and-occupation-account.lingua#r_1AYAC90C978F3DYTGSP2GRA1QT). The first four previously passed independent exact/high-precision, conservation, invariant, convergence and invalid-input checks recorded at their earlier checkpoints. They are unchanged here. Supporting mechanical verifications covered laminate force/moment/energy balance, rubber, weakest-link scaling, moisture, orthotropy, strengthening and corrected thermal shock.

The new independent-electron two-orbital periodic model uses bounded energies, nonnegative hoppings, positive thermal energy and a fixed mean filling. It returns the complete finite-ring spectrum, chemical potential, mean electron count, electronic energy, entropy, upper-band population and gap. It does not claim to predict an actual material's conductance or include interactions/disorder/device boundaries. Verification extracted the program from its Record. Eighteen separately assembled real-space rings and Jacobi eigenspectra agreed; 70-digit occupation/energy/entropy calculations, 24 analytic isolated-orbital cases, particle-count and entropy bounds, hopping interchange, energy/temperature scaling, particle-hole complements, half filling, three independent heat-capacity derivatives and 38 invalid domains passed. Grid-refinement energy errors at 8/16/32/64/128 cells were 0.00130576, 9.25244e-6, 6.46090e-11, 2.22045e-16 and 2.22045e-16 eV per cell; refinement reaches floating-point precision rather than decreasing strictly forever. The displayed example has gap 0.848528137424 eV, μ=0, two electrons per cell, energy −2.34964329510 eV per cell, entropy 0.03573805634 kB per cell and upper-band population 0.00306080867 per cell. Oscillator capacity, intrinsic-level shift, conductivity, optical reflection and Debye-loss examples also passed independent checks.

Cargo check for the publication-enabled Ergon test and rustfmt passed without compiler warnings. Lingua checked 2,110 Hephaestus files containing 2,148 Records. The register remains 263 planning/general Records, sixteen Hephaestus groups and 136 pending subjects, with 2,108 unique explicit developed identities. The historical cross-umbrella pending count remains 184. All 27,238 local Lingua references resolve. Static selection/discovery verifies exactly 195 selected/reachable Records, 1,495 discovered documents, four three-study branches and preservation of all shared original continuations/imports. Original electromagnetism publication/relocation passed in 56.78 seconds and original thermodynamics in 151.87 seconds after the new shared assertions. Expanded materials publication/relocation passed in 899.51 seconds. It preserved the exact selected/reachable learning set, discovery from eight representative entrances, all twelve composed routes, source identities, external references, relocated learning locations and all five programs through export, moving the exported directory and reopening with a fresh Library. The original electromagnetic and thermal selections also retained their independent discovery behavior. All 230 execution-ledger links resolve, and diff whitespace checks passed. No source media, views, personal instances or new Trail features were introduced. ERG-122 remains pending and the full Hephaestus goal remains active.

The [fabrication and state-control section](../../ergon/hephaestus/engineering/materials-engineering/processing/From-a-required-material-state-to-a-process-chain.lingua#r_2YT5QPFN4WNNHJFV88PEW1NKJC) adds 37 learning Records, seven source Records and fourteen shared continuations. Process chains, yield/resource balances, scale and atmosphere lead through melt shaping, forming, heat treatment and surface integrity; polymer rheology, shaping, cure and composite consolidation; powder/slurry/green-body/drying/debinding/sintering; glass, cementitious and wood material processing; joining, coatings, vapor/atomic-layer deposition, patterning, additive manufacture, qualification and scale-up. Three advanced studies require thermal/powder, reactive-flow and layered-material investigations, with primary evidence, independent verification, changed-condition predictions, criticism and revision. The latest terminal is [Defend the processing and material-state account](../../ergon/hephaestus/engineering/materials-engineering/study/Defend-the-processing-and-material-state-account.lingua#r_64R9D1KEEENG63D9E88T0VBBFV).

The fourteen newly reused nodes are `mech-process-plan`, `th-lumped`, `metal-casting`, `metal-forging-rolling`, `metal-extrusion-drawing-sheet`, `mech-surface-finishing`, `fd-generalized-newtonian`, `fd-objective-models`, `fd-rheometry`, `fd-porous`, `metal-powder`, `metal-joining`, `metal-additive` and `mech-process-variation`. Their original text, assertions and imports were checked byte-for-byte after removing only the new materials assertion and explanatory continuation. No reverse Imports were added. Fluid dynamics adds its existing 179 documents to discovery, preserving the original source identities and selection boundary.

The seven new sources are MIT 3.044 Spring 2013, the Open University's complete Manufacturing introduction, Romberg/Kotula's 2023 rheo-Raman/cure study, Romberg/Paul Roberts/Snyder/Kotula's 2024 conversion-model comparison, NIST SP 1173 (VCCTL version 9.5), MIT 6.777J Spring 2007 microfabrication, and an atomic-layer-deposition source joining Puurunen's 2005 review with Strempel and colleagues' 2018 primary study. Official contents and selected full-text methods/passages were inspected. MIT 3.044 has compact notes, missing lecture 12 and canceled lecture 17; it does not include its commercial textbooks. MIT 6.777J lectures 2–3 contain 56 and 65 PDF pages, with separately credited/omitted figures. The NIST manuscripts contain 30 and 42 pages; subsequent retrieval of the second sometimes timed out. Their official-contribution statements differ from a blanket licence on every credited component. The 2023 dataset DOI is verified, but its actual files remain uninspected. VCCTL's 61-page guide is a historical model reference; no present service/install/run has been verified. Puurunen's 56-page VTT manuscript permits restricted reading/research/educational uses and retains AIP copyright; the later experimental article is CC BY. The introductory powder taxonomy is explicitly criticized where it suggests all powder routes require die compaction.

The existing Wood Handbook and ceramic breviary retain their identities and gain precise processing references. The complete 2021 handbook was retrieved only to `/tmp/materials-processing-wood-2021.pdf`, with text in `/tmp/materials-processing-wood-2021.txt`. It has 546 PDF pages versus the bibliographic 543; selected chapter 10 bonding/surface and chapter 13 moisture/drying/stress passages were inspected, not every page/table. The source now includes the official GovInfo preserved PDF. Ceramic forming, firing/debinding and shrinkage pages were inspected; their historical standards and ranges are not universal current acceptance specifications. The apparent Portuguese *Tópicos em Materiais Poliméricos* download contained only three cover/imprint/presentation pages and was not added as a full book. The NPTEL nonmetal-processing endpoint remains unreliable and was not used to claim an inspected complete text. All books, papers, software, datasets and media remain external references.

[An inspectable cure and thermal history](../../ergon/hephaestus/engineering/materials-engineering/computation/An-inspectable-cure-and-thermal-history.lingua#r_591T0QHNX8Y619RXNQGF3CNPHF) adds a sixth original standard-library Python example. It couples first-order Arrhenius conversion with a homogeneous constant-capacity thermal balance and piecewise-constant oven conditions. Integrating accumulated reaction exposure and external heat preserves the energy relation while recovering conversion with `expm1`. Fourth-order Runge–Kutta uses bounded input/state domains, segment-aligned steps, thermal/reaction step checks and a total-work limit. These computational checks do not establish convergence or physical validity; the model omits spatial gradients, autocatalysis, vitrification, shrinkage and degradation, and its hypothetical parameters are not a named resin's production schedule.

Verification extracts the code from its Record. Forty analytic three-segment constant-rate cases include coincident reaction/thermal rates and zero-rate/zero-transfer limits. Six independent 55-digit integrations evolve conversion and temperature as separate variables. Energy balance, conversion bounds/monotonicity, identical-hold splitting and 54 invalid domains passed. Endpoint-temperature errors for steps 4/2/1/0.5 seconds against a 0.0625-second reference are 3.24315e-7, 2.04706e-8, 1.28568e-9 and 8.05471e-11 K. The example ends at conversion 0.999690655286, temperature 304.318265575 K and external heat −143476.199930 J/kg; its step-sampled peak is 410.008802455 K. The peak is not asserted as an exact continuous maximum. Yield, sharp-front impregnation and isotropic shrinkage examples were independently checked.

Processing verification status: Cargo check, rustfmt, Lingua syntax and static selection/discovery passed. Lingua counted 2,154 Hephaestus files with 2,192 Records; the register retains 263 Records, sixteen groups, 136 pending subjects and 184 historical cross-umbrella pending scopes. There are 2,152 unique explicit Hephaestus identities. Publication/relocation passed for expanded materials in 1,026.04 seconds, original fluid dynamics in 286.25 seconds, mechanical engineering in 432.46 seconds and metallurgy in 313.17 seconds. Materials discovery from six representative entrances, the exact 246-Record selected/reachable set, all thirteen composed routes, source boundaries, external URLs, internal learning-reference locations and all six programs survived export, moving the export and reopening with a fresh Library. All 27,646 local Lingua references and 232 execution-ledger links resolve; diff whitespace checks passed. No source media, views, personal instances or new Trail features were introduced. All publication tests and temporary download processes have finished; none remains running. ERG-122 and the full Hephaestus goal remain active and unfinished.

The degradation/environment section now continues from the processing review through [From a manufactured state to a changing material](../../ergon/hephaestus/engineering/materials-engineering/degradation/From-a-manufactured-state-to-a-changing-material.lingua#r_7N670RYBBFA2B12T71PBYGTCBF). It adds 38 learning Records, ten source Records and eight shared continuations: `gc-redox-balance`, `gc-nernst`, `metal-electrode-kinetics`, `gc-corrosion`, `metal-corrosion`, `mech-tribology`, `metal-wear` and `fd-cavitation-dynamics`. All eight originals were compared byte-for-byte after removing only their new materials assertions and continuation paragraphs; original content, routes and Imports are preserved, with no reverse Imports. The route develops exposure histories, thermodynamics versus rates, electrode kinetics/transport, unequal areas, polarization evidence, passivity, local corrosion, environmental cracking/hydrogen, hot oxidation/scales, polymer aging/weathering, moisture/reaction–diffusion, glass/ceramic/concrete/wood deterioration, interfaces, atomic-oxygen exposure, wear/erosion/cavitation, coupled damage, protection, accelerated testing and service-life inference. Three sustained studies branch into corrosion, polymer life and mineral-material durability and converge at [Defend the degradation and service-evidence account](../../ergon/hephaestus/engineering/materials-engineering/study/Defend-the-degradation-and-service-evidence-account.lingua#r_3FBQS7VXVJBP1D8TGNGYGWR8DJ).

The ten new references are Cambridge aqueous corrosion, Lister's corrosion-current chapter, NPL corrosion/control and testing critique, Bhadeshia's hydrogen-prevention review, Ulrich/Glatzel/Galetz's oxide-kinetics primary article, Sung/Nguyen's NIST weathering protocol, Duan and colleagues' outdoor-life inference, Ciccotti's glass stress-corrosion review, the FHWA AAR Facts Book and NASA-HDBK-6024. Whole chapters/books/reviews and complete relevant NPTEL coursework are assigned progressively. Official metadata, contents and selected technical passages were inspected on 30 October 2026; source Records distinguish that inspection from a complete independent reproduction.

Cambridge's printable corrosion package was retrieved with Python after browser 403 responses. Its Faraday quantity jM/(ρzF) is taught as a thickness rate; activation Gibbs energy is distinguished from reaction Gibbs energy, and natural-log slopes from volts/decade. Lister's chapter has 37 pages; it does not establish access to his entire course. NPL's pump/valve guide (2020) and Turnbull's concentrated-brine SCC critique (2005) have 28 and 27 pages; they are authorized free readings with restricted copying/Crown copyright. The old broken NPL links are not used. Bhadeshia's 2016 review is author-hosted; its trapping/prevention interpretation is explicitly subject to material, temperature, loading and timescale evidence. Ulrich and colleagues' 2021 article is CC BY 4.0 and treats oxide mass-change discontinuities; learners must derive rate-constant dimensions for the chosen exponent convention rather than copying ambiguous units.

Sung and Nguyen's July 2015 NIST SP 1200-15 contains twenty PDF pages and explicitly lacks some proprietary predispersed nanotube-feed details. Surface ATR and bulk transmission observations remain distinct. Duan and colleagues' 32-page 2017 manuscript supports laboratory fitting, dynamic exposure histories, functional thresholds and separate outdoor validation; raw files/full fit reproduction were not verified. Its arXiv distribution licence is not a general adaptation licence. Ciccotti's 18-page 2009 review distinguishes competing glass crack-growth mechanisms and the extrapolation consequences of similar local fits. FHWA-HIF-13-019, March 2013, by Thomas, Fournier and Folliard, lists 211 pages and supplies a whole-book AAR investigation, with historical standards kept distinct from current specifications. The existing Wood Handbook and ceramic breviary retain their original UIDs and gain degradation assignments; selected chapter 14 opening/contents and ceramic section 5.6–5.7 contents/test structure were checked. Their complete tables and present product/regulatory applicability were not independently verified.

NASA-HDBK-6024 was checked against its official revision register: baseline 2014, Change 2/revalidation 16 December 2022, 207 pages, publicly accessible and not designated a mandatory NASA standard. Contents and selected mass-loss/depth, witness-fluence and dehydration passages were inspected. It provides a detailed atomic-oxygen laboratory/flight comparison, not a complete reactor or radiation-damage subject. All downloaded PDFs/text are only under `/tmp/materials-degradation-*`; books, media, data and software remain external references.

[An inspectable mixed-potential and material-loss account](../../ergon/hephaestus/engineering/materials-engineering/computation/An-inspectable-mixed-potential-and-material-loss-account.lingua#r_52QDVA9TVTEX94JA9YF8DBQ9E7) adds a seventh original standard-library program. Two fitted Tafel branches balance total anodic/cathodic currents with unequal areas and optional cathodic mass-transfer limitation. Reference potentials specify the branches and are not asserted to be equilibrium potentials. Slopes use natural logarithms. The supplied potential window must already have physical justification; an unbracketed root is rejected. Logarithmic arithmetic and monotone bisection avoid intermediate overflow, with bounded inputs, finite-output guards and a checked current residual. Faraday conversion gives mass loss and average uniform penetration, not pit depth. Passivity, distributed potential/resistance, evolving surfaces, multiple reactions and local chemistry are omitted explicitly.

Verification extracted this seventh program from its actual Record. Forty-eight general-slope no-limit cases agreed with an independent analytic solution; 72 finite-limit equal-slope cases agreed with an independently derived 70-digit quadratic; forty constructed unequal-slope roots passed. Area scaling/ratio, transport monotonicity, narrowed windows, exact endpoint roots, total-current and Faraday balances passed, as did 81 invalid-input cases. Limiting-current inequalities allow floating-point rounding at saturation. The hypothetical default returns E=−0.3876162108454885 V, I=6.99426613694e−6 A, anodic/cathodic densities 0.00699426613694 and 0.000699426613694 A/m², mass loss 2.02411487754e−12 kg/s and uniform penetration 2.57193758265e−13 m/s. Earlier six programs are unchanged.

Degradation verification status: Cargo check and rustfmt passed without compiler warnings. Lingua checked 2,202 Hephaestus files containing 2,240 Records; the register retains 263 planning/general Records, sixteen groups, 136 pending subjects and 184 historical cross-umbrella pending scopes. There are 2,200 unique explicit Hephaestus identities. All 28,071 local Lingua references resolve. Static discovery/selection confirms 265 own Records, 292 selected/reachable, 1,766 discovered documents, 63 shared steps and six three-study branches. Publication/relocation passed for expanded materials in 1,109.91 seconds, original chemistry in 55.55 seconds, mechanical engineering in 437.42 seconds, metallurgy in 318.48 seconds and fluid dynamics in 315.90 seconds. Materials discovery from six representative entrances, the exact 292-Record selected/reachable set, all thirteen composed routes, source exclusions, external URLs, relocated learning locations and all seven programs survived export, moving the export and reopening with a fresh Library. The register separately passed Lingua syntax checking. All 235 execution-ledger links resolve; all 48 new files passed whitespace/root-discovery checks and git diff whitespace checks passed. No source media, views, personal instances or new Trail features were introduced. All publication tests and temporary download processes have finished; none remains running. ERG-122 and the full Hephaestus goal remain active and unfinished.

The selection/service/recovery section is now authored from `mat-degradation-review` through `mat-selection-review`: forty learning Records, eight source Records and eight shared continuations. It develops property provenance and procedural meaning, constraints and geometry freedoms, derived tie/beam/stability indices, multiple constraints, shape/process coupling, functional/interface selection, Pareto comparisons, uncertainty, substitution evidence and accepted-output costs. It then follows service state, inspection, failure, repair, reuse, separation, quality yields, polymer/mineral/wood recovery, functional equivalence, inventory modeling, allocation, lifetime, sensitivity and material stocks. Three advanced studies branch into structural selection, functional substitution and whole-life recovery, then converge. This does not finish ERG-122.

The reused steps are `mech-design-alternatives`, `mech-production-resources`, `mech-condition-evidence`, `mech-maintenance`, `mech-failure-investigation`, `mech-restoration`, `mech-recovery` and `metal-recycling`. Their source files receive only ordinary materials continuations and explanatory links, with their original routes and Imports retained. Root discovery remains thirteen subjects. The selection section passed publication/relocation; the final research extension has also passed publication and relocation checks.

Sources are MIT 3.080 (Kirchain, Fall 2005, Gregory's selection lectures), Munro's NIST SP 960-11, Tyler/Parnell's 2022 performance-indices booklet, DOE's August 2023 materials-selection report, ILCD's March 2010 detailed LCA guide, Raabe's 2023 sustainable-metals review, Thomas/Kneifel/Butry's NIST AMS 100-64 (October 2024), and McNeil/Kang's 2013 aggregate-recovery review. Existing UNEP and Portuguese CETEM metallurgy sources retain their identities. Source Records distinguish authorized reading from adaptation rights and actual inspected passages from complete assigned readings. MIT supplies only some homework and no lecture-5 uncertainty notes; proprietary software/data are not included. Munro's scanned PDF has 136 PDF pages, 128 bibliographic pages. DOE has 87 PDF pages; ILCD 417; NIST plastics 117. No PDFs, datasets, software or media are stored in the repository.

Research inspected official metadata, contents and selected technical passages. DOE section 2.2 and selected pallet/B-pillar discussions were read; tables 23–24 on printed page 52 were checked visually. The same-property assumption for primary/secondary variants is retained as an assumption to criticize, not qualified material evidence. The Ansys table on PDF page 4 was also checked visually; all learning indices are independently derived from stated geometry freedoms. ILCD allocation section 7.9.3, uncertainty annex 16.2 and selected representativeness/interpretation passages were read. Raabe sections 6.3.4 and 6.3.11 were inspected for impurity inheritance and coupled recovery; numerical thresholds remain conditional. McNeil/Kang's public article and CC Attribution statement were checked, with its historical standards kept distinct from current specifications. NIST plastics contents and introductory scope were inspected; its complete underlying process studies were not independently reproduced.

A new worked beam-selection comparison uses hypothetical states, not actual alloy grades. It requires independent curvature/energy derivations, constrained sizing, discrete gauges, changed loading and a declared simple service-loss extension. Seven earlier embedded Python examples remain unchanged. The section also gives explicit sequential recovery-yield, functional inventory, finite-horizon replacement and material-cohort balances, with their conventions and limitations.

The final research extension now continues from `mat-selection-review`. Thirty-two learning Records and six source Records develop specimen/history identity, measurement operators, identifiability, homogenization, closure, atomistic potentials, DFT, numerical precision, molecular dynamics, Monte Carlo, free energy, scale transfer, phase-field verification/coupling, calibration, held-out predictions, uncertainty, descriptors, data leakage and discriminating observations. Three integrated studies examine manufactured metal, thermoset history and functional interfaces, then converge at criticism. Literature synthesis, question/design, the reused `rc-paper`, independent investigation, scholarly defense and revision lead to the completion Record. This demands twenty-four advanced studies plus independent research; it does not claim those future reader investigations were performed during authoring.

Research lead for primary polymer evidence: Cuthbertson and colleagues, *Characterization of polymer properties and identification of additives in commercially available research plastics*, Green Chemistry 26 (2024), 7067–7090, DOI 10.1039/D4GC00659C. Publisher search exposes the abstract and selected results: 59 commercial specimens, multiple characterization methods, lot-specific limitations, supplementary data and a BOTTLE Plastic Substrates Database. Repeated direct article opens and the laboratory portal failed; the DOI now redirects to `https://pubs.rsc.org/gc/article/26/12/7067-7090/851700`. Full methods, supplements, raw data and licence still need inspection before claiming reproduction. Schyns/Shaver's 2021 packaging-plastics review has verified metadata but full-text retrieval remains unreliable. These leads were not promoted into new source Records.

Selection verification so far: Cargo check and rustfmt passed without compiler warnings. Lingua checked 2,250 Hephaestus files containing 2,288 Records; the register separately checked with 263 Records. Static discovery and exact learning selection/reachability confirm 313 own/340 selected/1,814 discovered, 71 shared steps and seven three-study branches. All 28,506 local Lingua references resolve; 2,248 explicit Hephaestus identities are unique and separate from planning identities. Eight new shared files were compared byte-for-byte after removing only the added materials assertions and continuation paragraphs. All 48 new files passed whitespace/root-discovery checks; git diff whitespace checks passed. Pending counts remain 136 Hephaestus subjects and 184 historical scopes.

Independent selection arithmetic: forty beam cases were checked by numerical integration of the strain-energy derivative. A separate 60-digit monotone sizing calculation checked the hypothetical beam's stiffness bounds and direct stress/deflection feasibility. State A requires h≈0.02426427503 m, m≈3.275677129 kg; B requires h≈0.01709975947 m, m≈6.711655591 kg; C requires h≈0.04641588834 m and fails the 0.04 m envelope. The listed gauges choose 0.025 m for A and 0.02 m for B, with no feasible C. Sequential recovery losses of 200, 80, 108 and 30.6 kg leave 581.4 kg and close the 1,000 kg input. These independently checked examples do not establish real grade qualification.

Selection publication/relocation verification passed: expanded materials in 1127.96 seconds, original mechanical engineering in 413.42 seconds and original metallurgy in 291.02 seconds. All three processes finished successfully before any research-extension publication input was changed. The test checked all thirteen routes, six representative discovery entrances, source exclusions, URLs, relocated learning locations and seven unchanged programs. The register syntax session also finished successfully. Logs remain `/tmp/lince-materials-selection-{materials,mechanical,metallurgy}.log`.

Research for the final materials section now includes [MIT 3.320, Ceder and Marzari, Spring 2005](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-320-atomistic-computer-modeling-of-materials-sma-5107-spring-2005/pages/lecture-notes/) and its [five computational labs](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-320-atomistic-computer-modeling-of-materials-sma-5107-spring-2005/pages/labs/). Lecture/lab indexes and selected lab 2/4 text were inspected. The complete graduate sequence covers potentials, first principles, DFT, molecular dynamics, Monte Carlo, free energies and coarse graining. Lab 2 explicitly compares convergence of energies, differences and forces; lab 4 compares integration, finite-size sampling and melting approaches. Current Quantum ESPRESSO and LAMMPS documentation and GPL terms were checked; actual versions, potential files and changed historical exercises still need documentation for a reader’s execution. Some annotated slides are incomplete, although original slides are supplied. Do not claim the labs have been executed.

[Bosoni and colleagues' DFT verification study](https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/items/a24b2247-92a6-455c-920b-44187986d8be), Nature Reviews Physics 6, 45–58 (2024), DOI 10.1038/s42254-023-00655-3, has a 94-page accepted-manuscript-plus-supplement copy downloaded to `/tmp/materials-research-dft.pdf` and extracted text. Selected recommendation, reference-system and data/code passages were read. It compares numerical implementations under a specified theoretical model, not agreement with experiment. Many cubic benchmark structures are intentionally not stable natural materials. The repository metadata says all rights reserved, while the accepted manuscript explicitly applies CC BY to AAM versions; retain this distinction. Its [Materials Cloud archive](https://archive.materialscloud.org/record/2023.81) was inspected as metadata/file listing only. Some AiiDA files are gigabytes; no datasets were downloaded or reproduced. The interactive browser requires JavaScript. Exact input/protocol and pseudopotential versions matter.

[PFHub benchmarks](https://pages.nist.gov/pfhub/benchmarks/) supply conserved/nonconserved fields, coupled solidification/elasticity and manufactured-solution verification. Selected [spinodal specification](https://pages.nist.gov/pfhub/benchmarks/benchmark1.ipynb/) and [Allen–Cahn manufactured-solution specification](https://pages.nist.gov/pfhub/benchmarks/benchmark7.ipynb/) were read. The [NIST software licence](https://github.com/usnistgov/pfhub/blob/nist-pages/LICENSE.md) grants broad reuse with attribution/modification notices; the old repository is marked archived on 23 July 2026, so treat it as a dated reference, not a maintained dependency promise. Jokisaari, Voorhees, Guyer, Warren and Heinonen's [dendritic-growth/elasticity benchmark manuscript](https://tsapps.nist.gov/publication/get_pdf.cfm?pub_id=924430) was downloaded to `/tmp/materials-research-phasefield.pdf`; selected introduction and benchmark-scope passages were read. Its PDF says 15 April 2020; official journal metadata identifies publication on 15 June 2018, DOI 10.1016/j.commatsci.2018.03.015. The source Record distinguishes these versions. No benchmark simulation was run.

[AM-Bench 2018-01](https://www.nist.gov/ambench/amb2018-01-description) gives a useful integrated case linking powder/process history, residual strain, distortion and microstructure. The initial plan and selected [actual deflection procedure](https://www.nist.gov/ambench/chal-amb2018-01-pd) and [neutron-strain results](https://www.nist.gov/ambench/chal-amb2018-01-rs) were inspected. Actual ridge preparation and measurement count differ from the initial plan; use the results' procedure. Diffraction is averaged over finite gauge volumes, not a point strain. The [data index](https://www.nist.gov/ambench/benchmark-test-data) warns that some links are deactivated; deflection raw download links are not currently supplied on its page. Residual-strain dataset DOI 10.18434/mds2-3052 initially failed in the browser, but its official landing HTML, README, IN625 neutron table and measurement PDF were subsequently downloaded outside the repository. The geometry figure was visually inspected. All 204 table positions are finite and distinct; columns are X, Z, YY, XX, ZZ, with millimetre coordinates and dimensionless strain. The first-row conversion to microstrain was independently checked. The [2019 Phan and colleagues paper](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40192-019-00149-0) is subscription-only at the publisher; an [institutional UC manuscript](https://escholarship.org/content/qt4pc2s4fh/qt4pc2s4fh_noSplash_11501e46d713aecff9e09644a79da78a.pdf) was found but remains unread. The 2019 paper remains unread; no complete raw-data reproduction is claimed. The separate 2022 mesoscale-tensile paper, DOI 10.1007/s40192-023-00307-5, was downloaded in full (23 pages), with selected methods/uncertainty, conclusions and availability passages inspected. Author names were checked against its first page. Its DIC-code/raw-data restrictions remain explicit. The associated 2022 raw dataset was not inspected in full.

The six new sources are MIT 3.320, Bosoni and colleagues' DFT precision study, PFHub/Jokisaari benchmarks, AM-Bench 2018 residual-strain data, the 2022 tensile challenge paper and Ramprasad and colleagues' 27-page 2017 materials-informatics preprint. The informatics paper's descriptor, testing, uncertainty and adaptive-design passages were inspected; arXiv grants distribution, not a general adaptation licence. The teaching extensions explicitly test specimen/batch/history separation and conditional uncertainty. Existing cure/rheology and ALD source identities are reused. All source PDFs, software and datasets remain external references.

